  "verdict": {
    "question": "Does MJX beat envs/parallel_vector_env.py (MuJoCo-C worker processes) for WalkerRagdoll-v0?",
    "answer": "NO on this hardware, at every batch size measured, on CPU and on GPU.",
    "committed_path_env_steps_per_s_n32": 6694.0,
    "committed_path_re_measured_n32": 7029.0,
    "go_no_go_bar_3x": 20082.0,
    "best_mjx_cpu_env_steps_per_s": 31.6,
    "best_mjx_gpu_env_steps_per_s_as_configured": 1275.0,
    "best_mjx_gpu_env_steps_per_s_relaxed_euler": 4338.0,
    "why": [
      "MJX is dense: walker_ragdoll.xml has 138 geom pairs and all 138 are collision-tested and solved every mj_step, while MuJoCo-C measured ncon=3 in the same fallen state. The solver works on ~46x more constraint rows than the sparse path needs.",
      "The committed model is RK4, which costs 4 force evaluations per mj_step: measured at nworld=512 on the RTX 4070 Laptop, RK4+postconstraint gives 935 env-steps/s and Euler gives 3,828, so RK4 alone is a ~4x tax. Switching integrator changes the dynamics, so it is not a free tuning knob.",
      "mjx.rne_postconstraint, needed for cfrc_ext/impact_cost, costs ~24% at nworld=512 (935 -> 1,231 env-steps/s without it). mjx.step does not compute cfrc_ext, exactly like mj_step.",
      "MJX-CPU throughput is flat-to-worse with batch size (31.6 env-steps/s at nworld=32, 22.9 at 128): a vmap'd XLA graph on one CPU device does not gain from more worlds, it just divides the same throughput.",
      "MJX-GPU saturates near 1,275 env-steps/s from nworld=512 onward; even the relaxed-Euler best case at nworld=1024 reaches only 4,338, still 1.5x below the committed CPU path."
    ],
    "gpu_status": "Measured for real: WSL2 Ubuntu-24.04 with jax-cuda12-plugin on the RTX 4070 Laptop (8 GB, XLA_PYTHON_CLIENT_MEM_FRACTION=0.6). Native Windows jaxlib is CPU-only."
  },
  "conclusion": "MJX-GPU peaks at 1,275 env-steps/s as configured (RK4 + cfrc_ext), 4,338 with the dynamics changed to Euler; both are below the committed 6,694 env-steps/s MuJoCo-C process path.",
